{"name":"Blaze Orange","hex":"#fe6700","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blaze orange","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bright shade of orange."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blaze_orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Safety orange","blocks":[{"text":"Safety orange is a hue used to set objects apart from their surroundings, particularly in complementary contrast to the azure color of the sky. The high-visibility color is commonly used in hunting contexts and for construction site safety."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/94/Sign_Road_Work_Ahead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safety_orange","wikidata":"Q1091453"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/FEMA_-_38851_-_County_Road_crew_cleans_storm_drainage_ditches.jpg/500px-FEMA_-_38851_-_County_Road_crew_cleans_storm_drainage_ditches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/FEMA_-_38851_-_County_Road_crew_cleans_storm_drainage_ditches.jpg/1280px-FEMA_-_38851_-_County_Road_crew_cleans_storm_drainage_ditches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_